i'm joined by the shadow foreign  secretary lisa nandy um  lisa nandy i i want to talk about the uh  omicron variant first um the prime  minister's in reintroduced quarantine  measures we've now got contact tracing  again stricter rules on face masks  i'm guessing you support those measures  are they enough  yeah i want to thank the scientists who  spotted the danger with this new omicron  variant very quickly and the government  of course has announced a new package of  measures which we'll consider in  parliament on monday but when the prime  minister comes to parliament on monday  there are a number of outstanding holes  in our defenses that he's got to address  there still seems to have been very  little action on ventilation in public  buildings especially schools which is  going to cause major problems if we  think  the virus the new variant is as  transmissible as scientists are  suggesting there are still some  outstanding issues around travel the  government is saying everyone has to  have a pcr test on day two when they  arrive back in the uk but they're not  suggesting pre-departure tests for  people before they board flights there's  no advice for people about traveling  back from the airport on public  transport and there are very few checks  on the pcr tests that are done so these  are all things that for months the  government has been warned about and  when he comes to parliament on monday  these are things that he's going to have  to be able to answer  so these are generally speaking though  um issues of detail aren't they but you  aren't pressing for a return to working  from home uh or mandatory masks in  hospitality venues and so on  we've always said that math squaring in  crowded spaces and flexible working from  home where people can ought to be part  of plan a we've been watching covid  rates rise across the country for  several months now and we've been urging  the government to do this much more  quickly  we've always said as well that the  government ought to be guided by the  science and it would be one of the  questions we'll be asking on monday of  the prime minister is what are the  scientists advising about mask wearing  is this a political decision to try to  limit mass squaring only to public  transport and shops or are there good  reasons why we ought to consider  extending that in the end this is about  preventing the virus from getting out of  control more people dying the nhs being  overwhelmed and another lockdown taking  place and we've been keen to avoid that  since day one and that's why we want the  government to come to parliament on  monday and be able to answer those what  i think are fairly reasonable questions  it to me as though to some extent you  accept uh what lies behind uh what the  health secretary just been saying to me  which is that there's a kind of balance  to be struck between  um responding to new variants and making  sure we don't shut down uh life as it's  currently being um carried on  businesses and so on and so forth um in  essence  you're accepting the strategy that mr  johnson laid out yesterday  so there is a balance to be struck i  think the health secretary is right  about that we don't think the government  has been getting the balance right up to  now and that's why we support the  measures to bring in  greater use of masks mandatory masks in  some settings and we'll be asking  questions about whether the scientific  basis for that suggests that that needs  to go further we desperately want to see  them title up the travel restrictions  there's a real problem when for 18  months the government has been warned  that there are holes in those defenses  and still hasn't taken action to plug  what you rightly suggested are just  practical measures that could be dealt  with in order to stop the va the new  variant spreading around the country if  we have one big criticism of the  government's approach it's actually  about why these new variants are  emerging  we haven't met our promises that we made  this summer at the g7 to get the vaccine  rolled out to other parts of the globe  there is now sufficient vaccine in order  to ensure that it reaches almost every  adult in the world the problem is that  there is no global plan to get the  vaccine to people in places like south  africa and many of the surrounding  countries that have even worse rates of  vaccination and as the medical chief  medical officer has said to us many many  times when you have huge numbers and  rising numbers of cases of covid that's  when you're like more likely to see new  variants  emerging the government has been warned  about this the prime minister said that  we were leading the world on this on  saturday it is simply not true we're  lagging behind the european union we're  nowhere near  coming close to meeting the commitments  that he made this summer and until and  unless we start to show some global  leadership around this we're going to  keep seeing this reappearing  but this is a global issue isn't it i  mean the south south africans have been  saying in the last 48 hours that  uh the  number of vaccinations that are sitting  in the vaults i mean their numbers  between 100 million and 500 million  being quoted unused  is a scandal for the whole of europe and  north america it's not just a british  problem is it  well it's it's a british problem because  you can see  that we're now dealing with the omicron  variant on our shores  and it's having major implications for  the way that people live as well as for  the national health service so of course  you're right that it's a problem that  requires global cooperation but it  requires britain to do our part and  currently the uk is not delivering on  the promises that we made we said we'd  get 100 million vaccines out to the rest  of the world and then instead of doing  that we've delivered about 10  of that commitment and we've also  slashed aid to many of the countries  that need investment in their health  care systems in order to get the jabs  into people's arms the prime minister  knows this he knows that this is the  reason this lack of global leadership is  the reason why we're seeing new variants  emerge and so while we're keen to  support  measures to protect the british public  and protect the nhs you really have to  ask why the government hasn't got its  act together in order to tackle the  problem at source when in june the prime  minister was keen to wave a british flag  and say that that's precisely what he  was doing  there's been anger in southern africa at  the  block the stopping of flight  would you  have supported that had you been asked  i i think it's absolutely the right  thing to do to suspend flights from not  just south africa but surrounding  countries and i appreciate that that is  a very difficult thing when it happens  to countries i mean certainly you know i  had many constituents making  representations to me  when we had travel bans in place for  britain  for quite a long time britain's couldn't  fly to the united states for example  because of the high rates here and it is  a very difficult thing to accept but the  first duty of any government is to keep  its citizens safe i think these measures  are  sensible and  one of the questions that we've asked  the health secretary to address is  whether there are other countries that  ought to be added to that list countries  in the area in the region with  high rates of infection  and whether  the government is going to come forward  with ways to check on the pcr tests that  are being done when people return to the  uk we can't afford to keep making the  same mistakes that we've made for 18  months over and over again and leave  these big holes in our defenses at a  time when we're struggling to get on top  of an existing or new variant  let's turn to what also appears to be a  perennial  problem to which we haven't found a  solution that is the channel crossings  with the deaths this week um  everyone including you says that french  and british need to sit down work  together that's a given  but um  why hasn't that happened do you think  who's to blame here is it boris johnson  for being  overly provocative or is it emmanuel  macron for overreacting  well france blames britain britain  blames france the truth is that both  governments are engaging in a blame game  while children drown off our coastline  it's just simply unconscionable and any  responsible government on either side of  the channel would set aside those  differences and work together to deal  with what is a collective shared problem  that will only be solved together it's  not just that they failed to agree the  very pressing and urgent need to disrupt  the people smugglers as sajid javid was  just saying to you by working together  to have coastal protect patrols and  other measures it's also that we haven't  been able to work together to tackle the  things that are driving the movement of  people the asylum system across europe  and across the world has completely  broken down if you look at the countries  that people are fleeing from it's syria  it's afghanistan there are reasons why  people are on the move and the world has  been completely unable to come together  to deal with that the government is  writing open letters to the french that  they're releasing via social media  calling on the french to do things that  any responsible government would have  already done to get in place a mechanism  across europe to be able to return  people to safe third countries they  travel through to agree safe and legal  routes for people who we owe obligations  to to come here and sajid javid just  told you trevor that the government is  legislating to make that happen you  can't legislate  just to criminalize organizations like  the rnli who go out to try and help  people in the channel and think that  will solve the problem one of the people  on the boat that sank um  just a week ago was an afghan soldier  who said he'd given up hope of being  able to get to britain through a legal  route because although  the home secretary announced an  expansion of that afghan refugee  settlement scheme that scheme hasn't  even opened yet and that was three  months ago these these routes simply  don't exist and they won't exist for as  long as the government continues to  engage in a blame game with others and  doesn't do the hard yards of sitting  down round the table and agreeing how  we're going to tackle this together  let's talk about what um if you were  sitting down with the french what you  would actually be saying to them um can  i first understand what is labour's  priority here um and i'm talking about  about in general i'm talking about what  you would do this week is it  to make sure that nobody drowns which  would mean for example taking people  on board  british coast guard or navy ships  irrespective  of whether they had a genuine claim or  not or is it  making sure that we get uh the numbers  who are landing down which would imply  that what you want to do above all is  turn those boats back what what is  labour's actual priority what were the  instructions that your government would  give to the border force  well of course we want to stop people  drowning off our coastline it's simply  unconscionable that we've got this  happening and involving children as well  and people who we owe obligations to but  the way that you do that is twofold  firstly you don't criminalize those like  the rnli who are trying to assist them  or put wave machines in the channel as  pretty patel has suggested to simply  smash those boats out of the water you  do that by trying to disrupt a system  that is thriving on people's despair and  on criminality so we should be working  with the french to arrest those people  smugglers who are who are fueling this  and are providing unsafe passage for  people they're profiting from human  misery so we need joint french british  action to do that we should be  intercepting these boats we should be  arresting the people smugglers and we  should be stopping people from drowning  but then we ought to be working together  in order to deal with the with with the  bigger issue and let me just give you an  example i met with the pakistani  government at the height of the afghan  refugee crisis there are 1.4 million  afghan refugees in camps in pakistan  alone some of those are people to whom  britain owes obligations but there is no  route to get here at all at the moment  others are people that france could  potentially assist canada australia the  united states and other countries  besides but until we have a global  asylum system that is capable of  responding to that and sharing the the  strain of this together we are going to  see desperate people making desperate  crossings and we've got to deal with it  i get that but with respect  this is a real issue  for every single day from now  somebody who is running one of these  smuggling rings watching this interview  now will have taken from what you said  just now  that  if he is prepared to play chicken with  these people's lives a labor  administration would simply  say okay  we'll let this this one in  aren't you actually simply encouraging  the people smugglers to believe  that in the end  if they are prepared to put people in  these small boats  we will they will get across  well firstly let me speak directly to  your hypothetical people smuggler under  a labour government if you were to  continue with this reckless disregard  for human life we will work with  international partners to find you to  intercept you and to lock you up for a  very long time this is profiting from  human misery and it simply won't stand  we would open up safe and legal routes  with our international partners and urge  other countries to do the same to pull  the rug out from under the business  model that these smugglers  currently rely on of desperate desperate  people who have no other options and we  would work with our international  partners to tackle the underlying  drivers of conflict and insecurity  including climate change which is  forcing many  many people 26 million people to move  across the world unless and until we  have a government that is capable of  responding to the scale of the challenge  the only people  that benefit are the people smugglers  and the rest of us all lose  lisa andy thank you very much indeed for  your time

